# No needless variable reassignment

Do not copy a binding into a same-value alias (`const foo = bar` where `foo` is only used to dodge `T | undefined` on a module-level `let`).

Use the original name. If a closure can run after the binding changes, guard it:

```ts
// BAD — alias exists only because `client` is `LanguageClient | undefined`
client = new LanguageClient(...);
const languageClient = client;
void languageClient.start().then(() => {
  languageClient.onNotification(...);
});

// GOOD — same binding; guard where the value may have gone
client = new LanguageClient(...);
void client.start().then(() => {
  if (!client) return;
  client.onNotification(...);
});
```

Also forbidden: `const self = this`, `const that = this`, `const value = x` when `value` is never transformed, renamed for clarity across a real abstraction, or capturing a snapshot that can diverge.

Allowed: a new name that is a different concept (`const uri = document.uri`), a snapshot that must not see later writes, or destructuring that pulls fields out of an object.
